kely of the same origin as charroyer, possibly from a Vulgar Latin *carriāre or *carreāre, but "},{"parola":"corme","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":2,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"sorb, sorb-apple","termine_origine":"curmus","catena":[{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"curmus","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"curmi","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"cornum","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"la","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From Latin curmus (“beer”) from Gaulish curmi (“beer”); influenced by Latin cornum."},{"parola":"druide","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"druid","termine_origine":"druidae","catena":[{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"druidae","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*druwits","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*druwits","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Inherited from Old French druide (13th c.), borrowed from Latin druidae, from Gaulish *druwits, from Proto-Celtic *druwits (“wise person”). The Old French form is definitely a borrowing and not inherited. The question poses itself, however, whether the word was inherited since then or borrowed again"},{"parola":"gouge","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"gouge (groove)","termine_origine":"gulbia","catena":[{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"gulbia","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"eu","nome":"Basque","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"eu","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Inherited from Old French gouge, from Latin gulbia (Late Latin gubia), of Gaulish or Basque origins."},{"parola":"Limousin","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"ereditato","incerta":"no","significato":"native or inhabitant of the Limousin region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, France (usually male)","termine_origine":"Lemovicīnus","catena":[{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"Lemovicīnus","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Originally an adjective referring to the city Limoges, from Latin Lemovicīnus, adjective form of Lemovicēs, name of a Gaulish tribe in central France, most likely a reference to their elm bows and spears."},{"parola":"orteil","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"ereditato","incerta":"no","significato":"toe","termine_origine":"articulus","catena":[{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"articulus","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"ordiga","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From Middle French and Old French orteil, variant of arteil (“toe”), from Latin articulus (“knuckle”). Doublet of article, of learned origin. Cognate with Occitan artelh (“toe”), Catalan artell, Spanish artejo (both “knuckle”). The semantic development in Gallo-Romance as well as the French o- are b"},{"parola":"sagum","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"latinismo_dotto","incerta":"no","significato":"sagum","termine_origine":"sagum","catena":[{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"sagum","categoria":"latinismo_dotto"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"cel","nome":"Celtic","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*seg-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Latin sagum."},{"parola":"sapin","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"fir, fir tree","termine_origine":"sappinus","catena":[{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termi